Bubble Sort 

Optimal Solution

Approach:
Traverse the array from the end towards the beginning.
In each pass, compare every pair of adjacent elements.
If the current element is greater than the next element, swap them.
After one complete pass, the largest element moves to its correct position at the end.
Use a didSwap flag to check whether any swaps occurred during the current pass.
If no swaps are made, the array is already sorted, so stop early.

Idea: Repeatedly swap adjacent elements so that the largest unsorted element "bubbles up" to its correct position after every pass.

Time Complexity:
Best Case: O(n) (when the array is already sorted due to early stopping)
Average Case: O(n²)
Worst Case: O(n²) (when the array is sorted in reverse order)

Space Complexity: O(1)
Sorting is performed in-place without using any extra data structure.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> bubbleSort(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n = nums.size();

        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            bool didSwap = false;

            for (int j = 0; j <= i - 1; j++) {
                if (nums[j] > nums[j + 1]) {
                    swap(nums[j], nums[j + 1]);
                    didSwap = true;
                }
            }

            if (!didSwap) {
                break;
            }
        }

        return nums;
    }
};


Key Takeaway:

"Compare adjacent elements → Swap if needed → Largest element bubbles to the end → Stop early if no swaps occur."
